prompt: Load the `notes` skill from ~/.claude/skills/notes/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/notes/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[notes] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 8–14 sentences, covering both scenarios. Scenario A: the user runs `add note in /tmp/vault for: insight: wadler immutable doc trees [[wadler]]` and `/tmp/vault` is a git repo with an `origin` remote. Scenario B: the user runs `merge buffer in /tmp/vault` and the buffer contains 3 entries that classify cleanly. For each scenario describe (1) when the commit fires, (2) what gets staged, (3) the commit message shape, (4) whether a push happens, and (5) what the skill does if the vault has no `.git` directory or the user aborts mid-disambiguation.
expect_substr: [notes] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers eight behaviours. (1) Trigger — commit fires only AFTER all intended writes for the verb succeed; a capture aborted at the disambiguation question, or a buffer merge that errors partway, MUST NOT commit. (2) Staging scope — the skill stages ONLY the paths it wrote/modified during the verb (the new insight file, every auto-created topic stub like `topics/wadler.md`, and on a buffer merge the classified destinations PLUS the archived `scratch/buffer-<DATE>.md` PLUS the reset `scratch/buffer.md` PLUS the `inbox/merge-report-<DATE>.md`); it MUST NOT run `git add -A` and MUST leave any pre-existing dirty changes in the vault untouched. (3) Single-capture commit message — minimal, path-shaped (e.g. `insights/wadler-immutable-doc-trees.md`); the message is the path of the primary write, NOT a Conventional-Commits subject (`feat(...)`, `notes(insight): ...`) and NOT a generic `add insight: ...` or dated session message. (4) Buffer-merge commit — ONE commit at the end of the whole drain (not one per classified entry), shape `merge buffer (3)`, covering ALL the side-effect paths above. (5) Push — when `git remote` is non-empty, push after the commit with `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` so credential prompts fail fast; push failure surfaces a one-line warning but does NOT roll back the commit and does NOT error the verb. (6) No-remote — silent skip; commit lands, no push. (7) Non-git vault — `.git` missing means the skill writes the file and returns; NO commit, NO warning, NO offer to `git init`. (8) Read-only verbs — `show me my buffer`, `find notes related to X`, `list notes by topic Y`, `list recent <kind>` write nothing, therefore commit nothing. Wrong answers to flag: `git add -A` or otherwise sweeping unrelated dirty files; one commit per buffer entry; Conventional-Commits-shaped messages; pushing without a remote; auto-`git init` on a non-git vault; committing on a half-finished capture; committing on a read-only verb; rolling back the commit when push fails; omitting topic stubs or buffer-merge artifacts from the staged set.
tools: claude,kiro
